== Chapter XVII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The strength of sin shown from its resistance to the power of the law.]
]

#smallcaps[The] strength of any #emph[person,] or of a #emph[fortified city,] may well be measured by #emph[the opposition] it is able to withstand without being overcome. Suppose we hear of a city that has endured #emph[a long siege] by a powerful enemy and yet has not been taken or conquered, whose walls have endured heavy #emph[bombardment] and are not demolished. Though we have never seen the place, we conclude that it is #emph[strong,] if not impregnable.

This consideration will also show the power and strength of #emph[indwelling sin.] It is able #emph[to hold out,] and not only to #emph[live,] but also to secure its reign and dominion against very strong #emph[opposition] made to it.

I will give only one instance: #emph[the opposition made to it by the law.] That opposition is often #emph[great and terrible,] and always #emph[fruitless.] Sin bears all its assaults and is not overcome. There are several things in which the law #emph[sets itself against] sin and its power. For example:

#emph[First,] it #emph[uncovers] it. Sin in the soul is like a secret, #emph[wasting] disease in the body: its being #emph[unknown] and unnoticed is one great means of its prevailing. Or it is like traitors in a #emph[civil state,] who carry on their design vigorously while they lie hidden. Most men in the world know nothing of this #emph[sickness,] indeed this #emph[death,] of their souls. Though they have been taught something of the #emph[doctrine] of it, they know nothing of its #emph[power.] They do not know it in such a way as to deal with it as their deadly enemy. Whatever a man is told, he cannot be said to know that he has a #emph[wasting fever] if he loves his life and does not set himself to stop its progress.

This, then, the law does: it #emph[uncovers this enemy.] It convinces the soul that there is such a traitor harbored in its bosom. Rom 7:7: “I would not have known sin except through the law. For I would not have known lust if the law had not said, ‘Do not covet.’” “I would not have known it”; that is, fully, clearly, distinctly. #emph[Conscience] will raise something of a commotion about it, but a man cannot know it clearly and distinctly from that. Conscience gives a man such a sight of it as the blind man in the Gospel had at the first touch on his eyes: “I can see the people, but they look like trees walking around”\; dimly and confusedly. But when the law comes, it gives the soul #emph[a distinct sight of this indwelling sin.] Again, “I would not have known it”\; that is, its #emph[depths,] its root, the habitual inclination of my nature to sin, which is called #emph[lust] here, as it is by #emph[James] (ch. 1:14). I would not have known it, or not known it to be sin, except through the law.

This, then, the law does: it drags this #emph[traitor] out of its secret lurking places, the inmost recesses of the soul. When the law comes, a man is no longer ignorant of his enemy. If he will now perish by it, it is openly and knowingly. He cannot help saying that the law #emph[warned] him of it and uncovered it to him. Indeed, it raised a #emph[crowd against it] in the soul, of various affections, as an #emph[officer] does who discovers a #emph[thief] or #emph[robber] and calls out for help to arrest him.

Secondly, the law not only #emph[uncovers] sin, but shows it to be a very #emph[bad lodger,] dangerous, indeed destructive, to the soul. Rom 7:13: “Did that which is good” (that is, the law), “then, become death to me? Certainly not! But in order that sin might be exposed as sin, it produced death in me through what was good, so that through the commandment sin might become utterly sinful.” There are many things in this #emph[verse] that do not concern us at present. All I aim at is the #emph[showing up] of sin by the law. It is “exposed as sin”\; and it is shown in its own colors, and appears “utterly sinful.” The law makes the soul know the #emph[filth] and #emph[guilt] of this #emph[indwelling sin,] how great they are; how vile it is, what an abomination, what enmity against God, how hated by him. Whatever thoughts the soul had of it before, it will never again look on it as a #emph[small matter]\; and by this it is greatly taken aback.

It is like a man who finds himself somewhat ill and sends for a skilled #emph[physician.] When the physician comes, the man asks his judgment of the illness. The physician considers his condition and tells him, “Alas, I am sorry for you. The case is far otherwise with you than you imagine. Your #emph[disease is mortal.] It has gone so far, pressing on your spirits and infecting the whole #emph[mass of your blood,] that I fear you will live only a very few hours unless the most effective remedies are used.”

So it is in this case. A man may have some #emph[trouble] in his mind and conscience about #emph[indwelling sin.] He finds that all is not well with him as it should be, more from the #emph[effects] of sin and its continual outbreaks than from its nature, which he hopes to wrestle with. But now when the #emph[law] comes, it lets the soul know that its disease is #emph[deadly and mortal,] that it is utterly sinful, as being the root and cause of all his estrangement from God. In this way, too, the law proceeds against it.

Thirdly, the law #emph[judges the person,] or lets the sinner know plainly what he is to #emph[expect] on account of this sin. This is the law’s proper work; its #emph[uncovering] power is only #emph[preparation] for its #emph[judging.] The law is itself when it is on the throne. Here it does not mince matters with sinners, as we are used to doing with one another, but tells him plainly, “You are that man!” “You are the one in whom this utterly sinful sin dwells, and you must answer for its guilt.” And this, I think, if anything, should #emph[rouse a man] to set himself against it, and indeed to destroy it utterly. The law lets him know that on account of this sin he is liable to the curse and wrath of the great God against him. Indeed, it pronounces the sentence of #emph[everlasting condemnation] on him on that account. “Remain in this state and perish” is its language. It does not leave the soul without this #emph[warning] in this world, and so it will leave it without #emph[excuse] in the world to come.

Fourthly, the law follows up its sentence in such a way that it #emph[disquiets] and frightens the soul, and does not allow it to enjoy the least #emph[rest] or quiet while it harbors its sinful lodger. Whenever the soul has given in to sin’s commands and made provision for it, the law immediately #emph[flies at it] with the wrath and terror of the Lord and makes it quake and tremble. It will have no rest, but is like a poor beast with a deadly arrow sticking in its side, which makes it restless wherever it is and whatever it does.

Fifthly, the law does not stop there; it also #emph[slays] the soul (Rom 7:3). That is, by its conviction of the nature, power, and desert of this #emph[indwelling sin,] it strips the person in whom sin dwells of all the life of #emph[self-righteousness and hope] that he formerly kept himself going with. It leaves him a poor, dead, helpless, hopeless creature. And all this it does in pursuing its opposition to this sin. May we not now expect that sin’s power will be #emph[quelled] and its strength broken, that it will #emph[die] away under these blows of the law of God? But the truth is that its power and strength are such that it is quite otherwise. The #emph[poets] tell of one born of the earth who, when someone thought to kill him by throwing him on the ground, gained new strength from every fall and was more vigorous than before. So it is with all the falls and repulses the law gives to #emph[indwelling sin.] For:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, it is not #emph[conquered.] Conquest implies two things for the one conquered: first, #emph[loss of dominion]\; and secondly, #emph[loss of strength.] Wherever anyone is #emph[conquered,] he is stripped of both. He loses both his authority and his power. So the strong man armed, when he is overcome, is #emph[tied up,] and his #emph[goods] are plundered. But neither of these comes upon #emph[indwelling sin] through the assaults of the law. It does not lose one bit of its #emph[dominion] or #emph[strength] by all the blows given to it. The #emph[law cannot] do this (Rom 8:3). It cannot strip sin of its power and dominion; for he “who is under the law is also under sin.” That is, whatever power the law gains over a man’s conscience, so that he is afraid to sin for fear that its sentence and curse will fall on him, sin still #emph[reigns and rules] in his heart. That is why the apostle says (Rom 6:14), “Sin shall not have dominion over you#super[+], because you#super[+] are not under law, but under grace.” He plainly implies that however much a person is subject to the authority of the law, that will not exempt and free him from the #emph[dominion of sin.] Indeed, by all its work on the soul, the law, instead of freeing and releasing it from the reign of sin and bondage to it, by accident, and not by its own nature, greatly increases its misery and bondage, as the sentence of the judge on the bench against a criminal adds to his misery. The soul is under the #emph[dominion of sin,] and perhaps remains in its woeful condition in great security, fearing neither sin nor judgment. The law attacks him in this condition by all the ways mentioned before, and brings him into great trouble and perplexity, fear and terror; but it #emph[does not deliver him at all.] So it is with the soul as it was with the #emph[Israelites] when #emph[Moses] had delivered his message to #emph[Pharaoh.] They were so far from gaining liberty by it that their bondage was increased, “and they realized they were in trouble” (Exod 5:19). And we will see that sin acts like #emph[Pharaoh.] Finding its rule disturbed, it grows more #emph[outrageously oppressive] and doubles the bondage of their souls. It is not the work of the law, then, to #emph[destroy] sin, or to strip it of the dominion it has by nature.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Nor does sin lose any of its #emph[strength] through all these #emph[blows of the law.] It keeps both its authority and its force; it is neither destroyed nor weakened. Indeed,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it is so far from being #emph[conquered] that it is only #emph[enraged.] The whole work of the law only provokes and enrages sin, and makes it put out its strength, as it has opportunity, with more power, vigor, and force than before. The apostle shows this at length in Rom 7:9, 10, 11, 12, 13.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But you#super[+] will say, “Do we not see by experience that many are worked on by the #emph[preaching of the law] so that they give up many sins, amend their lives, and fight hard against the outbreaks of other corruptions they cannot yet mortify? It cannot be denied that the power and #emph[efficacy] of the law is great when it is preached and applied to the conscience in a fitting way.” I answer:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] it is acknowledged that the power of the law of God is very great and effectual. Great are the effects worked by it, and it will surely accomplish every #emph[end] for which God has appointed it. Yet the #emph[subduing of sin] is no part of its work; God has not designed it for that purpose. So it is no dishonor to it if it cannot do what is not its proper work (Rom 8:3).]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] whatever effects it has on #emph[some,] we see that in #emph[most] the power and prevailing of sin is such that the law makes no impression on them at all. May you not see men everywhere living for many years in congregations where the #emph[law is powerfully preached] and applied to the conscience for all the ends and purposes the Lord is pleased to use it for, without being moved by it once? They receive no more impression from its blows than an #emph[adamant] stone would from blows with a straw. They are neither #emph[convinced] by it, nor #emph[terrified,] nor #emph[awed,] nor #emph[instructed,] but remain deaf, ignorant, senseless, and secure, as if they had never been told of the guilt of sin or the terror of the Lord. Congregations are full of such people, who proclaim the #emph[triumphant power] of sin over the ministry of the law.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly,] when any of the effects mentioned are produced, it is not from the power of #emph[the letter of the law,] but from the actual efficacy of the Spirit of God putting forth his virtue and power for that end and purpose. And we do not deny that the Spirit of the Lord is able to restrain and quell the power of lust when he pleases; we have considered before some ways in which he is pleased to do so. But,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Fourthly,] in spite of all that may be observed of the #emph[power of the law] on the souls of men, it is still most evident that lust is not #emph[conquered,] subdued, or mortified by it. For,]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] though the #emph[course] of sin may be #emph[driven back] for a season by the ministry of the law, its #emph[spring] and fountain is not dried up by it. Though it withdraws and hides itself for a season, it is only, as I have shown elsewhere, to #emph[get] out of a storm and then return again. A #emph[traveler] who meets a violent storm of thunder and rain on his way immediately turns off his road to some house or tree for shelter. Yet this does not make him #emph[give up his journey]\; as soon as the storm is over, he returns to his way and his progress again. So it is with men in #emph[bondage] to sin. They are pursuing their lusts; the #emph[law] meets them in a storm of thunder and lightning from heaven, terrifies them, and hinders them on their way. This turns them out of their course for a #emph[season]\; they will run to prayer or amendment of life for some shelter from the storm of wrath they fear is coming on their consciences. But is their course stopped? Are their principles altered? Not at all. As soon as the storm is over, and the sense and terror that was on them begins to wear off, they return to their former course in the service of sin again. This was Pharaoh’s state #emph[again and again.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] at such seasons sin is not #emph[conquered] but #emph[diverted.] When it seems to fall under the power of the law, it is in fact only #emph[turned] into a new channel; it is not dried up. Suppose you set a #emph[dam] against the streams of a river, so that you let no water pass in the old course and channel, but it breaks out another way and turns all its streams into a new course. You will not say you have #emph[dried up that river,] though some who come and look into the #emph[old channel] may perhaps think the waters are utterly gone. So it is in this case. Perhaps the streams of sin run in open #emph[sensuality] and profaneness, in drunkenness and vice. The #emph[preaching of the law] sets a #emph[dam] against these courses. Conscience is terrified, and the man does not dare to walk in the ways he was engaged in before. His #emph[companions] in sin, not finding him in his #emph[old ways,] begin to laugh at him as one who is #emph[converted] and growing strict. Professors themselves begin to be persuaded that the work of God is on his heart, because they see his old streams dried up. But if there has been only a #emph[work of the law] on him, a #emph[dam] has been put to his course, but the #emph[spring of sin] is not dried up; only its streams are turned another way. Perhaps the man has fallen into other, more #emph[secret] or more #emph[spiritual] sins. Or if he is beaten off from them too, the whole strength of lust and sin will take up its residence in self-righteousness, and pour out by it streams as filthy as by any other way whatever. So in spite of the #emph[whole work of the law] on the souls of men, #emph[indwelling sin] will still keep #emph[alive] in them; and this is another evidence of its great power and strength.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I will touch on some other evidences of the same truth I am considering, but I will be brief with them. Next, then, the great #emph[efforts of men ignorant of the righteousness of Christ] to #emph[subdue] and mortify sin, which are all fruitless, show its great strength and power.]

Men who have no #emph[strength against sin] may still be made aware of the #emph[strength of sin.] The way they mostly come to that knowledge is by some prior sense they have of the #emph[guilt] of sin. Men have this by the light of their consciences; they cannot avoid it. It is not a matter of their choice. Whether they want to or not, they cannot help knowing that sin is evil, and such an evil as makes them liable to the judgment of God. This galls the minds and consciences of some so much that they are kept in awe and do not dare to sin as they would. Awed by a sense of the guilt of sin and the terror of the Lord, men begin to try to #emph[abstain from sin,] at least from the sins they have been most terrified about. While they have this design in hand, the strength and power of sin begins to show itself to them. They begin to find that there is #emph[something in them] that is not in their own power. For in spite of their resolutions and purposes, they #emph[still sin,] and in such a way that their consciences tell them they must therefore perish eternally. This sets them on #emph[their own efforts] to suppress the outbreaks of sin, because they cannot be quiet, or have any rest or peace within, unless they do. Now they are ignorant of the only way by which sin is to be mortified, that is, #emph[by the Spirit of Christ.] So they fix on many ways of suppressing it in their own strength, if not of killing it. In the same way, being ignorant of the only way by which consciences burdened with the guilt of sin may be pacified, that is, by the blood of Christ, they try in vain by many other ways to accomplish that end; for no man can obtain peace with God by any efforts of his own. We must look into some of the ways in which they try to suppress the power of sin that throws them into an unquiet condition, and into how insufficient those ways are for that end.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] they will promise and bind themselves by #emph[vows] to keep from the sins they have been most prone to, and so most perplexed with. The #emph[psalmist] shows that this is one great engine by which false and #emph[hypocritical] persons try to free and deliver themselves from trouble and perplexity. They make #emph[promises to God,] which he calls “deceiving Him with their mouths” (Ps 78:36). So it is in this case. Freshly galled with the guilt of some sin that, by the power of their temptations, they have perhaps often been overtaken in, they #emph[vow] and #emph[promise] that they will not commit that sin again, at least for some #emph[set period of time] that they fix. And some who claim to direct their consciences in this duty prescribe this course of action to them. Their conscience about this vow now makes them watch over themselves as to the outward act of the sin they are galled with. And so it has one of two effects: either they abstain from it for the time they set, or they do not. Suppose they do not, as seldom they do, especially if it is a sin with a special root in their nature and constitution, made into a habit by custom, and some fitting temptation is presented to them. Then their sin is increased, and their terror with it, and they are woefully discouraged from making any opposition to sin. So for the most part, after #emph[one] or two vain attempts, or perhaps more, knowing no other way to mortify sin than this way of #emph[vowing] against it and keeping that vow in their own strength, they give up #emph[all struggle] and become wholly the servants of sin, kept within bounds only by outward considerations, without any serious efforts to recover. Or #emph[secondly,] suppose they succeed in their resolutions, and abstain from actual sins for their appointed season. Commonly one of two things follows. Either they think they have #emph[done their duty well,] and so may now indulge their corruptions and lusts a little, at least for a season, and so they are entangled again in the same snares of sin as before. Or else they reckon that their #emph[vow and promise] has preserved them, and so they “sacrifice to their own dragnet,” setting up a righteousness of their own against the grace of God. This is so far from weakening #emph[indwelling sin] that it strengthens it in its root and principle, so that it may reign securely in the soul afterward. Or at the most, the best success that can be imagined for this way of dealing with sin is merely the #emph[restraining] of some of its outward outbreaks, which does nothing to weaken its power. So such persons, with all their efforts, are very far from being freed from the inward #emph[toiling, burning,] disquieting, perplexing power of sin. And this is the state of most men who are kept in #emph[bondage] under the power of #emph[conviction.] Hell, death, and the wrath of God are continually set before their #emph[consciences.] This makes them labor with all their strength against what in sin most enrages their consciences and most increases their fears, that is, its #emph[actual outbreak]\; for for the most part, while they are free from that, they are safe, though in the meantime sin lies in turmoil in the heart and defiles it continually. With running sores, outward #emph[repelling medicines] may skin them over and keep their corruption from coming out. But the outcome is that they make them #emph[fester] inwardly, and so, though perhaps not so #emph[foul] and offensive as before, they prove far more #emph[dangerous.] So it is with this repelling of the power of corruption by #emph[men’s vows] and promises against it. Perhaps the #emph[outward outbreaks] are restrained for a season, but the #emph[inward root] and principle is not weakened in the least. And most commonly this is the outcome of this way: #emph[sin,] having gained more strength and been enraged by its restraint, breaks all its bounds and takes the soul captive to all filthy abominations. As was observed before, this is the principle of most of the #emph[visible apostasies] we see in the world (2 Pet 2:19, 20).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Because of the #emph[hateful,] fierce, poisonous nature of this #emph[indwelling sin,] the Holy Spirit compares sinners to #emph[lions, bears,] and #emph[cobras] (Isa 11:6, 7, 8, 9). Now this is the excellence of gospel grace: it changes the #emph[nature] and inward principles of these otherwise wild and untamed beasts, making the #emph[wolf] like the #emph[young goat,] the #emph[lion] like the #emph[lamb,] and the #emph[bear] like the #emph[cow.] When this is done, they may safely be trusted; “a little child will lead them.” But these #emph[efforts of their own] do not change the nature at all; they only restrain the outward violence. A man who takes a #emph[lion] or a #emph[wolf] and shuts it up to keep it from ravaging, while its inward violence remains, may well expect that at one time or another it will break its bonds and return to its former ways of plunder and violence. Shutting them up, as we see, does not change their nature at all, but only restrains their #emph[rage] from doing open damage. So it is in this case. It is grace alone that #emph[changes the heart] and takes away the poison and fierceness that is in men by nature. Men’s #emph[efforts of their own] only hold them in check as to some outward outbreaks. But,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] beyond bare #emph[vows and promises,] with some watchfulness in keeping them by a reasonable use of ordinary means, men have put themselves, and some still put themselves, on #emph[extraordinary ways] of mortifying sin. This is the foundation of everything in the papacy that has “an appearance of wisdom” and religion. Their hours of prayer, their fastings, their walling and cloistering themselves up, their pilgrimages, penances, and self-torturing discipline all spring from this root. I will not speak of the #emph[countless evils] that have gone with these self-invented ways of #emph[mortification,] and how all of them have been turned into means, occasions, and advantages for sinning. Nor will I speak of #emph[the horrible hypocrisy] that clearly clings to most of those who observe them; nor of the #emph[superstition] that gives life to them all, which is riveted into the nature and constitution of some, fixed on others by deep-rooted prejudices, and taken up by others for worldly advantages. But I will suppose the best that can be made of it, and it will be found to be #emph[a self-invented design] of men ignorant of the righteousness of God, to put a check on this power of #emph[indwelling sin] we are speaking of. It is almost incredible what fearful #emph[self-wasting] and horrible sufferings this design has carried men into. And no doubt their #emph[blind zeal and superstition] will rise up in judgment and condemn the horrible sloth and negligence of most of those to whom the Lord has granted the saving light of the gospel. But what is the end of these things? The apostle gives us a brief account (Rom 9:31, 32). “They do not attain the righteousness they aim at”\; they do not reach conformity to the law; #emph[sin is not mortified,] and its power is not even weakened. What it loses in sensual, carnal pleasures it makes up with great advantage in blindness, darkness, superstition, self-righteousness and pride of soul, and contempt of the gospel and its righteousness. And it reigns no less than in the most profligate sinners in the world.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Lastly, the strength, efficacy, and power of this law of sin may be further shown from its life and being in the soul, in spite of the wound given to it at the soul’s first conversion to God, and the continual opposition grace makes to it. But this is the subject and design of another work.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It may now be expected that we should add here the special #emph[uses] of all this uncovering of the #emph[power, deceit, prevailing,] and #emph[success] of this great adversary of our souls. But as for the humility, self-abasement, watchfulness, diligence, and turning to the Lord Christ for relief that become those who find the power of this law of sin in themselves by experience, these have been mentioned and pressed as occasion arose throughout the whole preceding discourse. And as for the actual #emph[mortification] of it, I will only recommend to the reader for his direction another small treatise written long ago for that purpose. I suppose he may do well to consider it together with this one, if he finds that these things concern him.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[To the only wise God our Savior be glory and majesty, dominion and power, both now and forever.] Amen.]]
